{"title":"1993 AJHSME Problems/Problem 25","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AJHSME_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A checkerboard consists of one-inch squares.  A square card, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/7/15788e198a53ced87268aea78fcde68a84de92c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" /> inches on a side, is placed on the board so that it covers part or all of the area of each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> squares.  The maximum possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/b/cab546c65bcac03787a97402c23550d6461089af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 4\\text{ or }5 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 6\\text{ or }7\\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 8\\text{ or }9 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 10\\text{ or }11 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 12\\text{ or more}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"559\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AJHSME_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pythagorean_Theorem\" title=\"Pythagorean Theorem\">Pythagorean Theorem</a>, the diagonal of the square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/6/9766bf675c2c0a8feda0cbbbbbd80321d352bacc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{(1.5)^2+(1.5)^2}=\\sqrt{4.5}&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"225\" height=\"32\" />. Because this is longer than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />( length of the sides of two adjacent squares), the card can be placed like so, covering <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> squares. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/c/a5c7e14c81e1d992b4ce33665e2d6737f6d4a0ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{(E)}\\ 12\\ \\text{or more}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/9/34999b021fa37b5b02c76b26d7db3ec1fd8a0545.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] for (int a = -2; a &lt;= 2; ++a) {     draw((-2,a)--(2,a)); draw((a,-2)--(a,2)); } pair A,B,C,D; A=(0,sqrt(2.25)); B=(sqrt(2.25),0); C=(0,-sqrt(2.25)); D=(-sqrt(2.25),0); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,lightgray); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AJHSME_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a hre